Subject: iOS: the shell's engine reads ask for this machine's player online

Online round 2 (D-105). In an online match a client's own player is slot
g_NetLocalSlot (1 for the first joiner); the host's numbering is kept on every
machine (netplay.md "The shape"). The iOS reads the touch layer makes at the
frame boundary assumed player 0 / g_Vars.currentplayer:

- menuIosDialogIsOpen() (0019) asked g_Menus[0] only. A client's pause menu and
  the match's end screen open in g_Menus[g_NetLocalSlot], so the touch layer
  stayed in gameplay mode over them: no menu pointer, a tap on the end screen's
  arrows was a look drag, and the end screen could not be paged or closed by a
  finger. Found joining a macOS host from the lane-3 simulator.
- The 0021/0052 accessors (position, pause, aim mode, the gun's second
  function, the weapon wheel) read g_Vars.currentplayer, which outside the tick
  is whichever player the last pass left current. They now read the local slot
  by number, and the two that go through bgun/g_AmIndex make it current for the
  read and put the previous one back.

- dialogChangeItemFocusWithMouse() (upstream) only took the pointer for
  menu->playernum 0, while menuProcessInput() gives the mouse to NET_LOCAL_UI's
  player. On a client the pointer could press the focused row of its pause
  menu but never move the focus: the Control page's End Game could not be
  reached by a finger (nor, on desktop, by a mouse). NET_LOCAL_UI here too.
  Not iOS-guarded (offline NET_LOCAL_UI(pn) is pn == 0, the same test); one
  for upstream.

Offline nothing changes: g_NetMode is NETMODE_NONE and both fall back to what
they did. Everything but the focus-walk line is inside PLATFORM_IOS.

--- a/src/game/menu.c
+++ b/src/game/menu.c
@@ -1553,7 +1553,12 @@
 	s32 colwidth = 0;
 
 	// only allow mouse control of player 1 menus; ignore mouse if navigating with keyboard
-	if (menu->playernum != 0 || !g_MenuUsingMouse) {
+	// (iOS overlay 0059: online, this machine's player's menus - NET_LOCAL_UI,
+	// which menuProcessInput already uses for the mouse's buttons. A client is
+	// slot 1, and with player 0 hard-coded here its pointer could press the
+	// focused row of its pause menu and end screen but never move the focus,
+	// so End Game was out of a finger's - and a desktop mouse's - reach)
+	if (!NET_LOCAL_UI(menu->playernum) || !g_MenuUsingMouse) {
 		return false;
 	}
 
@@ -1606,6 +1611,17 @@
  */
 s32 menuIosDialogIsOpen(void)
 {
+	// Online, a client's own menus are its slot's (g_NetLocalSlot: 1 for the
+	// first joiner), not player 0's: its pause menu and the end screen open
+	// there, and with only g_Menus[0] asked the touch layer stayed in gameplay
+	// mode over them - no pointer, so the end screen could not be paged or
+	// closed by a finger (Online round 2, D-105). The Online pages themselves
+	// are player 0's on every machine, so both are asked.
+	if (g_NetMode != NETMODE_NONE && g_NetLocalSlot > 0 && g_NetLocalSlot < MAX_PLAYERS
+			&& g_Menus[g_NetLocalSlot].curdialog != NULL) {
+		return 1;
+	}
+
 	return g_Menus[0].curdialog != NULL;
 }
 
--- a/src/game/player.c
+++ b/src/game/player.c
@@ -9423,6 +9423,32 @@
 }
 
 #ifdef PLATFORM_IOS
+/*
+ * The player this machine's screen belongs to (Online round 2, D-105). Offline
+ * that is g_Vars.currentplayer, as it always was here. In an online match a
+ * client's own player is slot g_NetLocalSlot (1 for the first joiner), and
+ * outside the tick g_Vars.currentplayer is whichever player the last pass left
+ * current - so every read below asks for the local slot by number.
+ */
+static s32 playerIosLocalNum(void)
+{
+	if (g_NetMode != NETMODE_NONE && g_NetLocalSlot >= 0 && g_NetLocalSlot < MAX_PLAYERS
+			&& g_Vars.players[g_NetLocalSlot]) {
+		return g_NetLocalSlot;
+	}
+
+	return g_Vars.currentplayernum;
+}
+
+static struct player *playerIosLocal(void)
+{
+	if (g_NetMode != NETMODE_NONE && g_NetLocalSlot >= 0 && g_NetLocalSlot < MAX_PLAYERS) {
+		return g_Vars.players[g_NetLocalSlot];
+	}
+
+	return g_Vars.currentplayer;
+}
+
